<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <PropertyGroup>
        <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
        <RootNamespace>Vixen.Ui.Desktop</RootNamespace>
        <Description>
            The window, the device and the four steps of a frame, for an application whose whole
            content is a user interface. Vixen.Ui draws into a list and Vixen.Graphics puts a list on
            a screen; this is the loop between them, and it existed three times — in Samples/02, in
            the vixen-app template and in the editor's host — before it existed once.
        </Description>
        <PackageTags>ui;application;host;window;swapchain;desktop;vixen</PackageTags>
        <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>

        <!-- See the CompilerVisibleProperty below, which is where this is argued. -->
        <VixenShaderBindingsInternal>true</VixenShaderBindingsInternal>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" Visible="false" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <!--
            ⚠ **No Vixen.Engine and no Vixen.App, and the absence is load-bearing rather than
            incidental.** docs/plan/00 § Layer discipline calls the Vixen.Ui ⇸ Vixen.Engine boundary
            the single most important one in the codebase, and this assembly is the thing that used
            to make people cross it: an application that wanted a window and a frame loop had a
            choice between writing four hundred lines and referencing a host that drags a scene, an
            ECS world and a fixed-step accumulator behind it. Now it has a third option, and
            CheckArchitecture asserts that Samples/02-HelloUi still takes it.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Ui.Renderer\Vixen.Ui.Renderer.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Graphics.RenderGraph\Vixen.Graphics.RenderGraph.csproj" />

        <!--
            ⚠ **For `ControlTheme.Install`, and it is a real dependency rather than a convenience.**
            A browser ships a user-agent stylesheet and nobody calls that a layering mistake: without
            one a `<Button>` is an unstyled box, and every application that wanted the control set to
            look like the control set had to remember one line. It is a *user-agent* sheet, so
            everything an application loads afterwards out-specifies it by origin — see
            `UiApplicationOptions.InstallControlTheme`, which is how an application that wants none of
            it says so.

            `Vixen.Ui.Controls.Advanced` is deliberately NOT here: docking, trees and property grids
            are a second package, and an application that wants them installs `AdvancedTheme` through
            `UiApplicationOptions.Configure` — one line, in the assembly that already references it.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Ui.Controls\Vixen.Ui.Controls.csproj" />

        <!--
            Platform events turned into document events, and a docking host's request for a floating
            window turned into a real one. Both halves are needed here: a host with no PlatformInput
            dispatches nothing, and one with no PlatformWindowHost is a host that cannot open a
            second window — which is the difference between this and the three copies it replaces,
            two of which could not.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Platform.Ui\Vixen.Platform.Ui.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Platform.Desktop\Vixen.Platform.Desktop.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Graphics.Vulkan\Vixen.Graphics.Vulkan.csproj" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <!--
            ⚠ **The interface's shaders live here now, and they are Raven's rather than hand-written
            GLSL.** These eight modules were committed three times as `.frag` and `.vert` — Samples/02
            carried five, the vixen-app template eight, Vixen.Graphics.Golden.Tests the reference set —
            and `SharedUiShaderTests` existed because the copies had already drifted: `ui-box.frag` in
            two of them was sixteen lines short of the shadow path, so a shape asking for a soft
            shadow got a hard-edged box at full opacity, on one copy, silently.

            `Ui.rvn` is one source for all eight stages, compiled by this repository's own compiler
            rather than by whatever `glslc` happens to be on somebody's machine. The editor has been
            driven from a Raven `Ui.rvn` for some time; this is the same file with the three
            compositing stages — blur, colour matrix, mask — ported into it, so there is now one
            Raven source for the interface and one hand-maintained GLSL copy left, under
            `Vixen.Graphics.Golden.Tests`, which is the one the reference images were rendered with.

            An application that takes this assembly ships all eight without deciding to, which is the
            behaviour that matters: the four optional stages — image, blur, colour, mask — are the
            ones whose *absence* is a picture rather than a failure. Leave out `UiImage` and every
            faded subtree renders opaque; leave out `UiColour` and `grayscale` silently does nothing.
            Samples/02 wired four of the eight and had exactly those bugs.

            Regenerating: the CheckShaders gate does it, and the command to do one by hand is in
            Shaders/README.md — because MSBuild will not have the double dash a command line needs
            anywhere in an XML comment, which is the same reason the editor's shader README carries
            its own copy.
        -->
        <EmbeddedResource Include="Shaders\*.spv" />

        <!--
            ⚠ **The bindings generator, and the reason it is here rather than a table of numbers in
            `UiShaderLibrary`.** It turns each `.reflect.json` into the constants the host binds
            against — and the one that matters is which vertex attribute location each stage reads.
            That number is Raven's decision, not this assembly's: `StreamPlan` locates a stage's own
            parameters *after* the shader's streams, so `Ui.rvn`'s three streams put the four vertex
            attributes at 3..6 rather than 0..3, and adding a stream moves them again.

            ⚠ A wrong location is not a validation error. The pipeline binds nothing to that attribute
            and the stage reads whatever the driver left there — an interface drawn from uninitialised
            memory, on one driver, silently. Reading the number back out of the compiler's own output
            is what makes that unable to happen.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Shaders.Generators\Vixen.Shaders.Generators.csproj"
                          OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                          ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"
                          PrivateAssets="all" />

        <AdditionalFiles Include="Shaders\*.reflect.json" />

        <!--
            ⚠ **Internal, because `Vixen.Editor.Host` has a `Ui.rvn` with a `UiVertex` in it too and
            it references this assembly.** The generator names its class after the shader, so both
            would emit a public `Vixen.Shaders.Generated.UiVertexKeys` and the editor would not
            compile: CS0436, which this repository builds as an error.

            The two shaders are genuinely different — the editor's declares five stages and this one
            eight — so the right answer is not to share a file but to stop publishing constants nobody
            outside this assembly can use. These say where *these* modules put their attributes; an
            assembly that cannot load them has no use for the numbers.
        -->
        <CompilerVisibleProperty Include="VixenShaderBindingsInternal" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Vixen.Ui.Desktop.Tests" />
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
